    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented customerCode de-duplication in `ClinicServiceImpl.generateCustomerCode()` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`).\n\n**Behavior:**\n- Computes the base code via the existing deterministic `Owner.customerCode(postcode, telephone, lastName)` (format `<REGION>-<HASH8>`).\n- A `null` base (missing telephone/last name) is returned unchanged.\n- Collects the `customerCode`s of all *other* owners (excluding the one being saved, by id).\n- If the base code is free, it's used as-is.\n- On collision, it appends `-<n>` with the **smallest n \u2265 2** that is unique (e.g. `NSW-1A2B3C4D` \u2192 `NSW-1A2B3C4D-2`, then `-3`, \u2026), and returns the de-duplicated code.\n\nI also added the `java.util.stream.Collectors` import. The full suite (`AcceptanceTest.coreCustomerCodesStayUnique`, which asserts distinct owners always get distinct customer codes) passes \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS, Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0**.\n\nNote: as the test's own comment explains, a genuine hash collision can't be forced black-box (it needs an identical normalized telephone + last name, which telephone-uniqueness already blocks), so the test verifies the rule's guarantee (distinct codes) rather than exercising the `-<n>` suffix path directly.",
